# regressor.tkr — the compiler as its own full-stack regressor (0.3.1, regressor-principal
# design doc, C5). `[tests] regression` (teko.tkp) lists this file FIRST.
#
# IT BUILDS NOTHING, and that is the whole point of the CHANNEL layout (owner ruling 2026-07-26:
# *"tenho certeza absoluta que a grande maioria das regressões caberiam em um ou dois projetos no
# máximo"*). A variation in SOURCE needs a separate FILE inside one project; only a variation in
# BUILD CONFIGURATION earns a separate build. Every executable scenario this file used to carry
# therefore moved to the CHANNEL whose configuration it needs, and what is left here is the four
# DECLARATIVE R0 scenarios — living documentation of what the self-host build already proves.
#
# THE CHANNELS, and what each one costs on this host (measured, `teko: regressions … (N builds`):
#
#   * examples/regressions/bulk — the cross-namespace composition regressors and the whole language
#     corpus, one MEMBER NAMESPACE per scenario under a single build, selected by `TEKO_REGR_ENTRY`.
#     1 build. (Its codegen-stage KNOWN-STOP pin adds a second one the day the channel goes green:
#     a build that reaches codegen is one whose checker PASSED, so that pin can share with nothing.)
#   * examples/regressions/own_native — the own-backend corpus (F2/F5/F9): ONE program, and one build
#     per TARGET, which is the only axis on it that varies. 4 builds on this host: the host project
#     itself, plus the lone rows for x86_64-windows, wasm32-wasi and `host-os-arch`.
#   * examples/regressions/own_native/own_native_riscv64.tkr — the same program for riscv64-linux
#     under qemu, plus the retired lane's ELF cross-check. 1 build.
#   * examples/regressions/diagnostics — every checker-stage rejection, one source file per rejected
#     construct, ONE failing build reporting them all (the 0.3.1 all-diagnostics ruling). 6 builds:
#     that one, plus FOUR checker PRE-WALK stops that provably cannot share it, plus the
#     unsupported-target boundary, which needs a build whose front end SUCCEEDS.
#   * examples/regressions/parse_diagnostics — the same for the PARSE stage, which aborts before the
#     checker runs and so cannot share the checker channel's build. 1 build.
#   * examples/regressions/cwd_build — the `cd <project> && teko build .` invocation shape. 1 build.
#   * examples/regressions/crossmodule — the `package` (`.tkl`) dependency shape. 1 build.
#
# THE ARITHMETIC, stated so the next reader can audit it instead of re-deriving it. Fifteen builds
# today, and every one of them is a DISTINCT build configuration or a distinct failing stage:
#
#     host (bulk) · host (own_native) · x86_64-windows · wasm32-wasi · host-os-arch · riscv64-linux
#     dependency shape · in-place shape · checker-fail · parse-fail · unsupported-target-fail
#     + 4 × checker PRE-WALK fail
#
# Three of those fifteen are removable, and each names who removes it:
#
#   1. the FOUR pre-walk builds collapse into the checker-fail build the day the pre-walk passes
#      COLLECT instead of stopping at the first error — the all-diagnostics work, not this channel's.
#      Measured both ways in diagnostics.tkr's header: today, folding one costs 49 diagnostics.
#   2. the two HOST builds become one the day `bulk` compiles: `own_native`'s corpus is green and
#      `bulk` is red on a pre-existing monomorph stop, so merging them today would turn sixteen
#      passing scenarios red to save one build. That is a worse trade than the build is worth.
#
# That leaves TEN as the structural floor: eleven configurations minus the one host merge. Going
# below ten means deleting a target or a build shape that a scenario asserts, which is coverage loss
# wearing a build count's clothes.
#
# THERE IS NO INLINE CODE IN THIS FILE OR IN ANY `.tkr` (owner ruling: "está proibido inline code em
# tkr"). Every scenario names its source as a file a human can open, compile by hand and diff.
#
# THE BUILD NOBODY COULD SEE, found while counting: the snippet FOLD (`src/build/regr_group.tks`)
# builds its groups BEFORE the first scenario runs and adds their cost to the RUN total but never to
# any file's own totals — so a per-file audit summed to fifteen while the run reported seventeen, and
# the two missing builds were its. With the own-backend corpus a project, no row in the corpus is
# groupable any more (a project row carries no snippet), the fold plans nothing and builds nothing,
# and the per-file sum now equals the run total exactly. The fold's own reason to exist died with the
# backend axis: `regr_group_solo` has been unconditionally true since the `TEKO_BACKEND` selector was
# excised, and a solo group is precisely what the `(file, declared env)` cache already gives a
# `Given source` row for free — while ALSO serving the rows the fold refuses (an object check), which
# is the build it was costing. It is dormant, not deleted: that is a compiler change, not a channel
# one, and it is reported rather than taken here.
#
# THE F3 NULL-UNION MATRIX (regressor-principal-0.3.1.md §3.1) lives in the channels, not here: every
# shape × operation × recursion cell is a runnable member of `bulk`, and two cells are HONEST GAPS
# pinned as compile-fail scenarios rather than silently dropped — a slice whose ELEMENT is itself a
# null-union (`[]T` where `T = X | null`) is REJECTED today (the parser reads `[](` as a FUNCTION
# type; spelled through an alias it reaches codegen's "optional/slice inner type not yet supported"),
# and an `Iface | null` (interface-in-union) construction is a checker-level KNOWN-STOP. The day
# support lands each pin fails LOUDLY, forcing a conscious promotion to a positive scenario. FFI
# `ptr<T> | null`/`uptr | null` (§3.1 "FFI") is NOT authored — there is no existing in-tree
# `ptr<T>`/`uptr` USAGE pattern to model a snippet against safely; flagged for the architect/owner,
# not guessed.
#
# The root-project guard (`guard_no_project_build_at_root`) refuses a scenario here with no
# inline/explicit source — which is exactly why every scenario below is declarative.
Feature: R0 — the compiler is its own full-stack regressor (exe native, declarative).

  # The compiler is a native `binary` artifact, so R0 already IS the regressor of the "exe native"
  # artifact kind AND of the whole LANGUAGE. The curated regressors under examples/regressions cover
  # only what R0 structurally CANNOT exercise (other artifact kinds, targets, FFI) — never language
  # features (those live in the channels above + the checker `#test` suite).

  Scenario: the compiler compiles the whole language
    # verified-by: the seed builds gen1 from the umbrella sources (teko . -o bin) — the self-host build.


  Scenario: the compiler passes its own gate
    # verified-by: teko test . exits 0 (600+ checker/build #tests + the D4 coverage floors).


  Scenario: the compiler reaches a byte-identical fixpoint
    # verified-by: gen1 == gen2 byte-identical (the seed-compiled and self-compiled compilers agree),
    # NEVER gen3 — the two-generation fixpoint is the release invariant.


  Scenario: the emitted code agrees across targets
    # verified-by: the own_native channel compiles ONE program for the host, for riscv64-linux (run
    # under qemu), for x86_64-windows and for wasm32-wasi, and every target asserts the same exit —
    # plus `Then object well-formed` on each cross target. The arc own==C that this scenario also
    # asserted MORREU with the emissor de C (ruling do owner 2026-07-26): there is no second backend
    # left to differ against.
